 ## Operating characteristics
 
-*   **Time Complexity:** Expected number of steps is proportional to the ratio of the majorant to the average extinction coefficient. In sparse volumes with a loose majorant, this can be very high [S11, S13, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Minimal additional memory beyond the volume data structure and the majorant. No need for structured grids or precomputed transmittance tables [S1, book][S14].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On the HACC dataset (51 million particles), delta tracking path tracing achieved 1.44 MR/s, compared to 6.0 MR/s for ray casting, but with higher quality and unbiasedness [S2, book]. Rendering a cloud with delta tracking and Next Event Estimation took 248s for 10 samples per pixel [S11, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Unbiased, handles arbitrary heterogeneous media (including particle volumes and unstructured grids), no need for voxelization [S2, book][S14].
-*   **Limitations:** High variance in sparse volumes with loose majorants; many null collisions waste computation [S11, S13, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When unbiased rendering of complex, unstructured, or highly heterogeneous media is required, and performance constraints allow for higher sample counts [S2, book][S14].
-*   **When Not to Use:** For homogeneous media (use analytical transmittance) or when real-time performance is critical and bias is acceptable (use ray marching) [S14][S17, unofficial].
